TI  - Simulation for Skills-based Education in Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine.
PG  - 579-86
LID - 10.1513/AnnalsATS.201410-461AR [doi]
AB  - The clinical practice of pulmonary and critical care medicine requires procedural 
      competence in many technical domains, including vascular access, airway 
      management, basic and advanced bronchoscopy, pleural procedures, and critical 
      care ultrasonography. Simulation provides opportunities for standardized training 
      and assessment in procedures without placing patients at undue risk. A growing 
      body of literature supports the use and effectiveness of low-fidelity and 
      high-fidelity simulators for procedural training and assessment. In this 
      manuscript by the Skills-based Working Group of the American Thoracic Society 
      Education Committee, we describe the background, available technology, and 
      current evidence related to simulation-based skills training within pulmonary and 
      critical care medicine. We outline working group recommendations for key 
      procedural domains.
